#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068504 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "df0c3b59-7667-53e9-84d3-bba30ea116fb" :knowledge-point/title "Integrating a Logarithmic Expression Using Cyclic Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "5b83575b-9674-5dc5-b3eb-a918f145be93" :question/math-academy-id "e-497" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2{x}}{x} \\textrm{d}x$ using integration by parts." :question/worked-solution "First, we let\n\n$$\nI = \\int \\dfrac{\\ln^2 x}{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = \\ln^{2} x & ⇒\\frac{du}{dx} = \\frac{2\\ln x}{x}, \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dx} = \\frac{1}{x} & ⇒v = \\int\\frac{1}{x}dx = \\ln x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\int\\frac{\\ln^{2} x}{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\intu\\frac{dv}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= uv - \\intv\\frac{du}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= (\\ln^{2} x)(\\ln x) - \\int(\\ln x)(\\frac{2\\ln x}{x})dx \\\\\n&= \\ln^{3} x - 2\\int\\frac{\\ln^{2} x}{x}dx \\\\\n&= \\ln^{3} x - 2I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $I$, remembering to add a constant of integration $C$ at the very end:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\ln^{3} x - 2I \\\\\n3I &= \\ln^{3} x \\\\\nI &= \\frac{\\ln^{3} x}{3} + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "72d0b4b3-bfc6-5339-809f-ff7cd63e4d3a" :question/math-academy-id "e-498" :question/problem "Calculate $\\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin{x} \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "To use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = e^{x} & ⇒\\frac{du}{dx} = e^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dx} = \\sin x & ⇒v = \\int\\sin xdx =-\\cos x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\inte^{x}\\sin xdx \\\\\n&= \\intu\\frac{dv}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= uv - \\intv\\frac{du}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= e^{x}(-\\cos x) - \\int(-\\cos x)(e^{x})dx \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\cos x + \\inte^{x}\\cos xdx \\\\\n&=-e^{x}\\cos x + J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = e^{x} & ⇒\\frac{du}{dx} = e^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dx} = \\cos x & ⇒v = \\int\\cos xdx = \\sin x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\inte^{x}\\cos xdx \\\\\n&= \\intu\\frac{dv}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= uv - \\intv\\frac{du}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= e^{x}(\\sin x) - \\int(\\sin x)(e^{x})dx \\\\\n&= e^{x}\\sin x - \\inte^{x}\\sin xdx \\\\\n&= e^{x}\\sin x - I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our equation for $I$, we can subsequently solve for $I$. We remember to add a constant of integration $C$ at the end.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= -e^{x}\\cos x + J \\\\\nI &= -e^{x}\\cos x + e^{x}\\sin x - I \\\\\n2I &= -e^{x}\\cos x + e^{x}\\sin x \\\\\nI &= \\frac{e^{x}}{2}(\\sin x - \\cos x) + C\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068568 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d1da25ef-5d1b-588f-b1cc-121562ff98ea" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Definite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/id #uuid "f415cae9-6b18-53c1-8c47-6e0a1f0de59c" :question/math-academy-id "e-7313" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int_0^{\\pi/2} e^x\\sin(2x) \\, \\textrm{d}x$." :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the indefinite integral by\n\n$$\nI = \\displaystyle \\int e^{x}\\sin(2x)\\, \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo use integration by parts, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = e^{x} & ⇒\\frac{du}{dx} = e^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dx} = \\sin (2x) & ⇒v = \\int\\sin (2x)dx = - (\\cos (2x))/(2)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= \\inte^{x}\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\intu\\frac{dv}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= uv - \\intv\\frac{du}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= e^{x}(- (\\cos (2x))/(2)) - \\int(- (\\cos (2x))/(2))(e^{x})dx \\\\\n&= - (e^{x}\\cos (2x))/(2) + \\frac{1}{2}\\inte^{x}\\cos (2x)dx \\\\\n&= - (e^{x}\\cos (2x))/(2) + \\frac{1}{2}J\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we can use integration by parts to evaluate\n\n$$\nJ = \\int e^{x}\\cos(2x)\\,\\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nTo do this, let's suppose that\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}u = e^{x} & ⇒\\frac{du}{dx} = e^{x}, \\\\ \\frac{dv}{dx} = \\cos (2x) & ⇒v = \\int\\cos (2x)dx = (\\sin (2x))/(2)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nUsing the integration by parts formula once more, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nJ &= \\inte^{x}\\cos (2x)dx \\\\\n&= \\intu\\frac{dv}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= uv - \\intv\\frac{du}{dx}dx \\\\\n&= e^{x}((\\sin (2x))/(2)) - \\int((\\sin (2x))/(2))(e^{x})dx \\\\\n&= (e^{x}\\sin (2x))/(2) - \\frac{1}{2}\\inte^{x}\\sin (2x)dx \\\\\n&= (e^{x}\\sin (2x))/(2) - \\frac{1}{2}I\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, substituting this into our equation for $I$, we can subsequently solve for $I$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI &= - (e^{x}\\cos (2x))/(2) + \\frac{1}{2}J \\\\\nI &= - (e^{x}\\cos (2x))/(2) + \\frac{1}{2}((e^{x}\\sin (2x))/(2) - \\frac{1}{2}I) \\\\\nI &= - (e^{x}\\cos (2x))/(2) + (e^{x}\\sin (2x))/(4) - \\frac{1}{4}I \\\\\n\\frac{5}{4}I &= (e^{x}\\sin (2x) - 2e^{x}\\cos (2x))/(4) \\\\\nI &= (e^{x}(\\sin (2x) - 2\\cos (2x)))/(5)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate our definite integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{π/2}e^{x}\\sin (2x)dx &= \\left.((e^{x}(\\sin (2x) - 2\\cos (2x)))/(5))\\right|_{0}^{π/2} \\\\\n&= (e^{π/2}(\\sin (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2}) - 2\\cos (2 \\cdot \\frac{π}{2})))/(5) - (e^{0}(\\sin (2 \\cdot 0) - 2\\cos (2 \\cdot 0)))/(5) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2e^{π/2}}{5} + \\frac{2}{5} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{5}(e^{π/2} + 1)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b428331b-4916-5b2c-8aa6-89a2358cdef0" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Indefinite Integral of a Exponential-Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cda7138-9604-5f7a-b22a-9a860832a0f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(3\\cos (3x) - \\sin (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065753} {:answer/id #uuid "be7de30e-b7ff-5ce3-bba5-5ba9874a66f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(3\\sin (3x) - \\cos (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065754} {:answer/id #uuid "169d6c83-6b3a-57ea-8bc3-2476ae6215f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}\\cos (3x) - 3\\sin (3x))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065755} {:answer/id #uuid "45eeec82-53f4-5815-be3b-5b8eab689391" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}3\\cos (3x) - e^{-x}4\\sin (3x))/(5) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065756} {:answer/id #uuid "7444d42a-42fb-53f5-a3bc-978b57e668f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(\\sin (3x) + 3\\cos (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065757}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be7de30e-b7ff-5ce3-bba5-5ba9874a66f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(e^{-x}(3\\sin (3x) - \\cos (3x)))/(10) + C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065754} :answer-field/id #uuid "29e101d3-f204-5a57-a1ea-d89637f4ea5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186065752}] :question/id #uuid "f0920268-6184-5022-affb-309ac870c06c" :question/math-academy-id "q-14878" :question/problem "Calculate $\\inte^{-x}\\cos (3x)dx$."}]}
